<p class="lede">Concurrency is designed but not in the compiler: every program in this book runs one step at a time. It shows what the compiler says today, the planned shape, and the habits that carry over.</p>
<note>Planned: not in the compiler yet. Scopes, spawned tasks, joining, mutexes and channels.</note>

<h2 id="today">What happens if you try</h2>
<p>Earlier drafts used <code>async</code> functions and <code>await</code> to wait; revision 0019 replaced that design with structured concurrency: <code>scope</code> blocks and <code>spawn</code>. Neither the old keywords nor the new design run in the compiler today; the old keywords fail to parse:</p>
<pre><code class="byn">async i64 fetch(i64 n) {
    return n + 1
}

void main() uses console {
    print($"{await fetch(1)}")
}</code></pre>
    <figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN1002:</span> expected an expression, found keyword `async`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> async-try.byn:1:1

<span class="gut">  1 │</span> <span class="hl">async</span> i64 fetch(i64 n) {
<span class="gut">    │</span> <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>

<span class="err">error BYN1002:</span> expected an expression, found keyword `await`
<span class="gut">  --&gt;</span> async-try.byn:6:14

<span class="gut">  6 │</span>     print($"{<span class="hl">await</span> fetch(1)}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>              <span class="hl">^^^^^</span>

<span class="gut">✗ build failed: 2 errors, 0 warnings</span>
<span class="gut">  explain: bayan explain BYN1002</span></pre>
      <figcaption>Real output from the compiler. The path is shortened to <code>async-try.byn</code>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
<p>The new design is not implemented either: <code>scope</code> and <code>spawn</code> are unrecognized, and <code>Mutex</code> and <code>Channel</code> are unknown types (BYN2002).</p>
<p>Two parts are accepted but do nothing: <code>uses suspend</code> in a function's effect list (<a href="book-14-effects.html">chapter 14</a>) and <code>#:io evented</code> at a file's top. Nothing can suspend, so neither changes your program.</p>

<h2 id="design">The planned shape</h2>
<p>Three ideas.</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Waiting is an effect.</strong> A function that can suspend says <code>uses suspend</code> in its header.</li>
<li><strong>Tasks live in a scope.</strong> Spawn tasks inside a <code>scope</code> block with <code>spawn</code>; the scope waits for every child before it exits, so no task outlives its caller, and <code>.join()</code> waits for one. The first failure cancels the siblings, and so does a <code>timeout:</code> or <code>until:</code> deadline on the scope. Children may borrow from the caller's stack.</li>
<li><strong>Shared data has rules.</strong> Shared mutation goes through <code>Mutex&lt;T&gt;</code>, atomics or channels; holding a mutex guard across a suspend point is a compile error, and passing <code>ref x</code> to a spawned task locks <code>x</code> in the parent until the scope ends.</li>
</ol>
<pre><code class="byn">// planned
i64 prepareIftar(String host) uses suspend fails NetError {
    return 10
}

i64 totalMeals() uses suspend fails NetError {
    scope(timeout: 5s) {
        let aisha = spawn prepareIftar("Aisha")
        let bilal = spawn prepareIftar("Bilal")
        return try aisha.join() + try bilal.join()
    }
}</code></pre>
<p>Treat it as a sketch, not code to copy; the <a href="cookbook-concurrency.html">concurrency cookbook</a> lists each planned form with its place in the specification.</p>

<h2 id="habits">Habits that already carry over</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep data owned by one place and pass it by <code>move</code> or <code>ref</code> (<a href="book-13-memory.html">chapter 13</a>): the task rules build on these.</li>
<li>Declare effects in <code>uses</code> clauses (<a href="book-14-effects.html">chapter 14</a>): waiting will be one more effect there.</li>
<li>For parallel work today, run several processes; the <a href="cookbook-processes.html">processes cookbook</a> shows how.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="quiz">Check your understanding</h2>
<details class="quiz"><summary>1. You write <code>async i64 fetch(i64 n)</code>. What does the compiler report today?
<ol type="a"><li>A run-time panic.</li><li>BYN1002, expected an expression, found keyword <code>async</code>.</li><li>Nothing; it runs as ordinary code.</li></ol></summary>
<p>b. A keyword from an earlier draft, rejected by the parser: <code>scope</code> and <code>spawn</code> replaced <code>async</code> and <code>await</code>.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>2. You add <code>uses suspend</code> to a function. Does it now run concurrently?</summary>
<p>No: accepted in a declaration, but nothing can suspend; it only says the function may wait.</p></details>
<details class="quiz"><summary>3. Why does the design say a task may borrow from its caller's stack?</summary>
<p>The scope waits for every child, so no child outlives the borrow: chapter 13's rule that a borrow must not outlive what it points into.</p></details>

